  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1157 AM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nGradual warming and dry conditions will dominate through mid to late \nweek. An aggressive upper shortwave will move to just offshore of \nthe Pacific Northwest Monday, becoming a closed H544 low by this \npoint. It will broaden and travel parallel to the West coast through \nmid week before moving into California by Thursday. Upper ridging \nwill amplify during this time over the central CONUS and will, for a \nshort period, slow and almost stop the eastward progression of the \nupper low. This upper ridge will allow for a warming trend with \nhighs reaching around 80 by Wednesday. Models remain mixed over the \nevolution of the upper low by Friday into the weekend. While they \nare similar with having the bulk of the upper low phasing with the \nmain flow, that is where the similarities end. The GFS holds on to \npart of the upper low across the Desert Southwest which eventually \nabsorbs the remnants of Hurricane Trudy in the Pacific, leading to \npotentially heavy rainfall by early next week. The ECMWF loses hope \nwith the upper low and phases the entire low with the main upper \nflow and tries a scenario similar to the GFS except using a new, \nfresh upper low.",
